  abstract = {Difference-in-Differences (DiD) is a useful statistical technique employed to estimate the effects that exogenous events can have on the outcome of some response variables. The latter are obtained from a random sample of treated units (i.e. units exposed to the event) ideally drawn from an infinite population. The comparable random sample/s of untreated units serve as control comparison group/s. The event is termed ``treatment'', but it could be equally termed ``causal factor'' to emphasise that with DiD we are not estimating a mere statistical association among phenomena. With DiD we try to evaluate whether a precise causal link between causes and effects --defined according to a model based on a proper identification of the relationship among variables-- is actually consistent with the data, and to estimate how intensive and statistically robust the causal-effect link actually is.},
